  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1019 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nWV imagery showing mid-level (500mb) low over far SW Arizona and \ncontinuing to slowly drift northeast. Deeper layered moisture \nremains north and southeast of the CWA, with less layered moisture \nover the CWA. That said, KEPZ sounding shows most moisture sitting \nbetween 700mb and 500mb, while dewpoints are back into the lower 40s \nmost places. So some light showers continue across southern portions \nof our forecast area this evening. Modest instability down over \nnorthern Mexico has allowed a few thunderstorms to develop in that \narea. As they drift northeast toward our southern CWA, they appear \nto weaken to just a few showers. \n\nMonday and Tuesday...sub-tropical jet increases in strength and \napproaches New Mexico from the southwest. Models showing one leading \njetlet from the main jet moving over southern New Mexico through \nTuesday. Also the main trough from the Arizona low nears the AZ/NM \nstate line later Monday as the parent low lifts out over the Four \nCorners. This added large scale lift will combine with PWs mostly \nunchanged from today, and dewpoints increasing to mid to upper 40s, \nto aid in isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms both days. \nCoverage may lessen some on Tuesday with slight drying aloft and the \nupper trough passage.  \n\nWednesday and Thursday...next large Pacific low approaches SoCal \nWednesday night and 90+ kt sub-tropical jet moves over western New \nMexico by Thursday. This will draw sub-tropical moisture over \ncentral Mexico northward to the area, perhaps including some \nmoisture from Trudy. Max PWs occur from Wednesday night through \nThursday night, approaching 1.2 inches, which is well over 200% of \nnormal. Thus expect good shower/thunderstorm coverage to develop \nWednesday afternoon. It could very well last much of the overnight \nhours and continue much of Thursday. MUCAPES of 300-600 J/kg along \nwith decent veering profile, show decent potential for some strong \nstorms. Main threat though could be local heavy rainfall and \nflooding. \n\nFriday through Sunday...Friday looks to begin transition down as the \nmain upper trough lifts across the area. Areas from the RG Valley \neast could still see decent coverage of showers/storms, but should \ntaper off to the west. From there the GFS/ECMWF continue to diverge \nsignificantly in solutions. The GFS continues to break off secondary \nlow over the northern Baja Saturday and Sunday, keeping sub-tropical \nmoisture and widespread showers again by Sunday. The ECMWF is much \nmore progressive, with little or no secondary low development. Thus, \narea should remain dry, though ECMWF does begin moving moisture back \nin after this period.",
